## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced architectural decision-making under production constraints; evaluates understanding of efficient attention patterns, tokenization strategies, and deployment optimization techniques.

## Key Concepts

- **Attention strategies**: Full vs sparse approaches, global tokens for maintaining long-range dependencies
- **Positional encodings**: Rotary vs learned embeddings for effective length extrapolation
- **Tokenization**: BPE fundamentals, domain adaptation techniques, code-switching handling methods
- **Deployment**: Quantization strategies, paged caching implementations, asynchronous batching approaches

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: windowed attention with global tokens
function hybridAttention(tokens, globalTokens, windowSize) {
  const localAttention = computeWindowedAttention(tokens, windowSize);
  const globalAttention = computeGlobalAttention(tokens, globalTokens);
  return combineAttentions(localAttention, globalAttention);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical, end-to-end RAG pipeline with real-world constraints: chunking, embedding stability, vector DB tenancy, latency targets, and robust failover. It also probes how to evaluate recall/latency at scale across multiple tenants.

## Key Concepts

- Chunking strategy: 512-token blocks aligned to maintain context.
- Embeddings: stable models (SBERT or OpenAI Ada) with versioned updates.
- Vector DB tenancy: per-repo namespaces and strict access controls.
- Freshness: index aging, re-embedding on document updates.
- Latency: retrieval latency targets (p95/p50) and caching.
- Failover: lexical BM25 fallback and provenance surface.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: split text into 512-token chunks using a simple tokenizer (word-based placeholder)
function chunkDoc(text) {
  const tokens = text.split(/\s+/);
  const chunkSize = 512;
  const chunks = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < tokens.length; i += chunkSize) {
    chunks.push(tokens.slice(i, i + chunkSize).join(' '));
  }
  return chunks;
}
```

