## Why This Is Asked
Enterprise chat assistants must process multilingual, long-context inputs containing specialized domain terminology while maintaining performance. This question evaluates understanding of tokenizer design, attention mechanisms, and practical engineering trade-offs in production systems.

## Key Concepts
- Multilingual subword tokenization with domain-specific vocabulary enhancement
- Sparse attention patterns using local windows and global anchors
- Cross-language semantic alignment and retrieval augmentation
- Latency-accuracy trade-offs in large-scale deployment

## Code Example
```javascript
function buildSparseMask(n, localWin, globalAnchors) {
  const mask = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(false));

  // Local attention windows
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const start = Math.max(0, i - localWin);
    const end = Math.min(n, i + localWin + 1);
    for (let j = start; j < end; j++) {
      mask[i][j] = true;
    }
  }

  // Global anchor attention
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (const anchor of globalAnchors) {
      mask[i][anchor] = true;
      mask[anchor][i] = true;
    }
  }

  return mask;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of retrieval-augmented NLP basics using a concrete tech stack (MongoDB Atlas Vector Search) and a beginner-friendly workflow.

## Key Concepts
- Chunking strategy for long docs
- Embedding models and cosine similarity
- Vector database indexing and search
- Simple result assembly and error handling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example chunking
function chunkText(text, maxChars=512){
  const words = text.split(/\\s+/);
  let chunk = '', chunks = [];
  for (const w of words){
    if ((chunk + ' ' + w).length > maxChars){
      chunks.push(chunk.trim()); chunk = w;
    } else {
      chunk += ' ' + w;
    }
  }
  if (chunk) chunks.push(chunk.trim());
  return chunks;
}
```

